 Behavior constants in `config.rs`: `MAX_ANVILS = 3` (parallel ingot slots), `HIGH_GRADE = 3` (plan-model threshold), `MAX_ITERATE = 3` (self-iteration rounds).
 
-`DuelMode::Auto` duels grade 3 and up, and only when `model_alt` differs from `model_base`. With everything on `openrouter/auto` that gate stays shut, so the default never pays 3x tokens to duel a model against itself.
+`DuelMode::Auto` uses work shape and grade to select one, two, or three casts for independent ingots. Grades 3-4 usually use two casts, grade 5 or taste-heavy work uses three, and mechanical work stays single-cast. `:casts`, `:duel`, and `SLAG_DUEL` override the choice. Direction prompts distinguish casts, so `model_alt` may match `model_base`.
 
 ### Commands
 
diff --git a//Users/stas/Playground/slag/README.md b//Users/stas/Playground/slag/README.md
index ea665cf2955a7d16fa776f13979b5af66046ec17..632fd0acee3aa818715355fce1cd88b7ca185125
--- a//Users/stas/Playground/slag/README.md
+++ b//Users/stas/Playground/slag/README.md
@@ -144,7 +144,7 @@
 
 Independent ingots (`:solo t`) run on up to 3 parallel anvils. Dependent ingots (`:solo nil`) run sequentially after.
 
-High-grade ingots can go to a twin-cast duel: two casts build the same ingot in separate git worktrees, a judge model assays both, and the winner merges. Duels only fire when cast B runs a different model from the worker, since two rolls of the same dice cost triple the tokens and buy nothing. Set `SLAG_MODEL_ALT` to a different family to turn duels on for grade 3 and up, or force the behavior with `SLAG_DUEL=on` / `off`.
+Eligible independent ingots use two or three casts in separate git worktrees, with minimal, robust, or creative direction prompts. A judge model assays passing casts, then slag merges the winner. Auto mode uses work shape and grade: grades 3-4 usually use two casts, grade 5 or taste-heavy work uses three, and mechanical work stays single-cast. `:casts`, `:duel`, and `SLAG_DUEL` can override the choice. Cast B may use the same model as cast A.
 
 ### Phase 4: ASSAY
 
